    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d392">

                <pb facs="0611" n="509" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg509"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">504.418B1/218</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Marriner</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2242</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-10-14">October 14,
                        1935</date>.<lb/>[Received October 25.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In continuation of my despatch No. 2179 of
                    September 19, 1935,<anchor n="12" xml:id="d392-f1"/> I have the honor to report
                    that the Embassy has again made inquiry of the competent official at the Foreign
                    Office concerning the French Government’s attitude toward the Roerich Pact or
                    Convention. The official stated that his Government has further investigated the
                    matter and is less inclined now than was the case last month to give serious
                    consideration to the Pact. He added that the legal advisers of the Foreign
                    Office had expressed the opinion that the Pact is not worthy of serious
                    consideration, at least at the present moment.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Theodore Marriner</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
